{
  "index": 4,
  "text": "With the development of an aging society, frailty is gradually becoming a public health problem threatening human health. Frailty is a state characterized by reduced physiological reserve and loss of resistance to stressors caused by accumulated age-related deficits [55]. There is an increasing agreement that indicators of frailty encompass age-related declines in lean body mass, strength, endurance, balance, walking ability, and low activity levels, and that frailty is defined in clinical by the presence of multiple contributing factors [1]. Frailty may also trigger and exacerbate other health issues [2], such as the elevated rates of mortality [3], hospitalization [4], and disability [5]. In the long term, frailty can be effectively treated or prevented.",
  "style": "Normal",
